const GRC_POLICY = "EBM Supplier Governance, Risk and Compliance Policy (MOCK)\nDocument GRC-PRC-007 \u00b7 Version 3.2 \u00b7 Owner: Procurement and Internal Control \u00b7 Effective 1 July 2026\n\nThis is mock content for a demonstration. It is not an EBM policy.\n\n1. Purpose and scope\nThis policy sets how a new supplier is screened, scored and approved before a purchase order can be raised. It applies to every supplier of ingredients, packaging, logistics and services. Food-contact suppliers carry the strictest checks because a single bad lot reaches the consumer under our brand.\n\n2. Supplier categories\nRaw materials: flour, sugar, edible oils and shortening, cocoa, dairy, flavours and any other ingredient. All are food-contact.\nPackaging (food contact): laminates, wrappers, trays and liners that touch product.\nPackaging (secondary): cartons, shippers, labels and pallets.\nLogistics and warehousing: primary and secondary transport, cold chain, third party warehouses.\nServices and indirect: maintenance, IT, consultancy, facility services.\n\n3. Knockout rules\nA supplier that triggers any knockout is Blocked. A Blocked supplier cannot be approved unless the Head of Procurement records a written exception with the reason.\nK1 \u2014 No valid NTN, or the supplier is not on the FBR Active Taxpayer List.\nK2 \u2014 Food-contact supplier (raw materials or food-contact packaging) without a valid food safety certificate: FSSC 22000, ISO 22000, BRCGS or HACCP. An expired certificate counts as no certificate.\nK3 \u2014 Ingredient supplier without a valid Halal certificate from PHA, PSQCA or a body they recognise. An expired certificate counts as no certificate.\nK4 \u2014 The supplier, its directors or a company sharing directors appears on the EBM Restricted Parties list in section 6, or on a UN, OFAC or NACTA sanctions list.\nK5 \u2014 A relationship between the supplier's owners or directors and an EBM employee that the supplier did not disclose on the form.\nK6 \u2014 Credible evidence of child labour, forced labour or bonded labour.\n\n4. Scoring criteria\nEach criterion is scored 0 to 5. The weighted score is the sum of (score \u00f7 5 \u00d7 weight) and runs from 0 to 100.\nC1 \u2014 Legal and tax compliance (weight 15)\n5: NTN, STRN and SECP registration all present and consistent. 3: NTN present, STRN or SECP missing with a reasonable explanation. 1: registrations inconsistent or unverifiable.\nC2 \u2014 Food safety and quality certification (weight 20)\n5: FSSC 22000 or BRCGS, valid for 6+ months. 4: ISO 22000 or HACCP. 3: ISO 9001 only, for non-food-contact suppliers. 0: none for a food-contact supplier.\nC3 \u2014 Financial stability (weight 15)\n5: 10+ years trading and turnover above PKR 250 million. 3: 3 to 10 years or turnover PKR 50 to 250 million. 1: under 3 years and turnover below PKR 50 million, or asks for full advance payment.\nC4 \u2014 Operational capacity and delivery (weight 15)\n5: stated monthly capacity covers EBM's volume at least twice over, with named large clients. 3: capacity covers EBM's volume. 1: capacity unclear or below EBM's volume.\nC5 \u2014 Ethics, labour and environment (weight 10)\n5: SMETA or Sedex audit plus ISO 14001. 3: one of the two, or a credible written policy. 1: nothing on record.\nC6 \u2014 Reputation and public record (weight 15)\n5: clear web presence, positive or neutral coverage, no adverse news. 3: little or no web presence, nothing adverse. 2: no web presence and the supplier gave no website. 0: adverse news about fraud, adulteration, litigation or regulatory action.\nC7 \u2014 Commercial terms and references (weight 10)\n5: 60+ day credit terms and verifiable references. 3: 30 day terms. 1: advance payment only, no references.\n\n5. Tiers and approval authority\nTier A, score 80 and above: approve. Category buyer may approve.\nTier B, score 65 to 79: approve with conditions recorded in the decision note.\nTier C, score 50 to 64: send back for remediation; ask the supplier for the missing items.\nTier D, score below 50: reject.\nBlocked, any knockout: reject, or approve only with a Head of Procurement exception.\nA supplier expected to exceed PKR 50 million in annual spend also needs Head of Procurement sign-off. Decisions are due within 5 working days of the form being received.\n\n6. Restricted parties\nSunrise Agro Industries (Pvt) Ltd, Sheikhupura. Debarred March 2024 after a shortening lot failed adulteration tests (palm stearin blended with animal fat). Directors at the time: Tariq Mehmood Butt, Naila Tariq. Any company with either director is restricted until March 2029.\nFaraz Traders, Karachi. Debarred August 2023 for issuing fake sales tax invoices. Proprietor: Faraz Ahmed Qureshi.\nBright Pack Industries, Lahore. Suspended January 2025 for repeated ink migration failures on wrappers. Review due January 2027.\n\n7. Current approved suppliers (extract)\nWheat flour: two approved mills. Procurement wants a third source for Faisalabad region.\nShortening and oils: one approved supplier. Single-source risk; a second source is a priority.\nCocoa powder: one approved importer. Second source wanted.\nFood-contact laminates: three approved converters. No new supplier needed unless price or quality is clearly better.\nLogistics, Karachi to Lahore: two approved carriers. A third is wanted for peak season.\n\n8. Monthly volumes used for capacity checks\nWheat flour 1,800 tonnes. Shortening 450 tonnes. Cocoa powder 60 tonnes. Laminates 120 tonnes. Karachi to Lahore loads 90 trucks.\n";
